          </p>

          <p>
            The label declares milk and soy. The soy comes from soy lecithin, a small amount used to help the powder dissolve, and most people with a soy intolerance tolerate it, but anyone with a diagnosed soy allergy should treat the warning seriously. Whey isolate is low in lactose rather than lactose-free, and Rule 1 does not make a lactose-free claim for R1 Protein. If you have a milk allergy, kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication that affects protein or potassium intake, check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily shake.
