        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Rule 1 Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, per Rule 1: every protein page we checked, including R1 Protein, Whey Protein, Casein, Source 7, Clear Whey Isolate and Plant Protein, states that the product is Informed Choice certified and "regularly tested for banned substances by the world class sports anti-doping lab, LGC." Informed Choice is LGC's certification program, which samples products for substances on the World Anti-Doping Agency's prohibited list.
          </p>

          <p>
            We tried to confirm the listing in the Informed Choice registry at choice.wetestyoutrust.com, but the search page would not load for us on November 21, 2026, so treat this as a brand claim we could not independently verify. If you are a drug-tested athlete, search the registry yourself for the exact product and flavor, and check the batch number on your tub where the registry lists batches. Rule 1 does not claim NSF Certified for Sport, so do not assume it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know what the certification covers. Informed Choice tests for banned substances, which is what matters for athletes, but it is not a heavy-metal or label-accuracy certification. We did not find a published heavy-metal result for R1 Protein from the Clean Label Project or a similar group, and Rule 1 does not post its own. Whey isolate tends to test lower in heavy metals than plant and chocolate-heavy powders, but that is a category pattern, not a result for this product.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other trust signal is the label itself. Rule 1 publishes a full Nutrition Facts panel and ingredient list for every flavor of every protein on its site, and R1 Protein's 25 g of protein per 29.5 to 32.5 g scoop adds up for an isolate-based powder. The brand also states that its amino acids are naturally occurring, which is the claim you want when a label shows high EAA and BCAA numbers. Nothing on the panel looks padded.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Informed Choice means banned-substance testing, not purity testing</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It is the right badge for athletes who are drug tested. It says nothing about heavy metals, and Rule 1 has not published that data for R1 Protein.</p>
          </div>
