        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Is Rule 1 a Good Protein Powder? Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rule 1 is a good protein powder for most people who want whey: R1 Protein delivers 25 g of protein per scoop from isolate and hydrolyzed isolate, publishes a full label for every flavor, claims Informed Choice testing and costs about $1.69 per 25 g in the big tub. That combination of a clean isolate label, wide flavor choice and mid-range price is hard to fault.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Rule 1 if</h3>

          <p>
            You lift or train regularly and want a lean shake with almost no fat or sugar. You are cutting and want 25 g of protein for 110 to 120 calories. You compete in a drug-tested sport and want a product carrying Informed Choice (confirm the listing yourself). You want variety: few isolates come in 19 flavors plus Unflavored. Or you want the Vanilla Ice Cream flavor, in which case buy the Whey Protein blend knowingly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip Rule 1 if</h3>

          <p>
            You avoid artificial sweeteners: the flavored R1 Protein panels all list sucralose and acesulfame potassium, so look at the Unflavored or naturally flavored versions, or a stevia-only brand. You need a dairy-free or vegan powder, in which case Rule 1 Plant Protein or our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> picks are the place to look. You have a soy allergy. Or you only ever buy small tubs, where Rule 1's Amazon price per gram is ordinary.
          </p>

          <p>
            A final note on who should check with a professional first. A daily shake is safe for most healthy adults, but people with kidney disease, pregnant or breastfeeding women, people with a milk allergy and older adults managing medical conditions should ask a doctor or registered dietitian how much protein suits them. For everyone else, R1 Protein is one of the safer bets in the whey aisle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Whey Protein Blend When You Wanted R1 Protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both tubs carry the R1 logo, but only R1 Protein Whey Isolate is concentrate-free. The Whey Protein blend adds whey concentrate, gums, 2 g of fat and 80 mg of cholesterol per scoop. If lactose or a lean label is why you chose Rule 1, read the product name on the front before you check out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Amazon Prices During a Brand-Site Sale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On November 21, 2026, the 2 lb R1 Protein tub was $59.99 on Amazon and $51.99 on Rule 1's own site, a gap of about 13%. Rule 1 runs frequent sales, so compare both before buying, and factor shipping thresholds into the brand-site total rather than looking only at the sticker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Scoops Instead of Grams of Protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                R1 Protein scoops range from 29.5 g (Unflavored) to 32.5 g (Milk Chocolate), and the blend's scoop is 33 to 35 g for 24 g of protein. Tub weight and serving counts mislead. Divide the price by total protein in 25 g portions, and the cheapest-looking tub is not always the best value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Informed Choice as a Purity Certificate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Informed Choice screens for substances banned in sport. It does not test for heavy metals or confirm the protein number on the label. Rule 1 has not published heavy-metal data for R1 Protein, so if that is your concern, look for a product with a published Clean Label Project or similar result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Whey Isolate Is Lactose-Free or Allergen-Free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                R1 Protein is low in lactose, not lactose-free, and Rule 1 makes no lactose-free claim. Every panel declares milk and soy, the soy coming from soy lecithin. People with a true milk allergy should avoid it entirely, and very lactose-sensitive drinkers should start with half a scoop to see how they react.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
